╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Analysis: since RZ gates and CZ gates are both diagonal in the computational basis, they  │
│                          │ mutually commute. This means the current block's `rz_pre_i` and `rz_post_i` layers        │
│                          │ (separated only by a chain of CZ gates) are mathematically equivalent to a *single*       │
│                          │ combined RZ rotation per qubit (rz_pre_i + rz_post_i) — i.e. half of the 16 RZ parameters │
│                          │ are redundant and contribute no extra expressive power, just wasted parameter budget and  │
│                          │ extra circuit depth.                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: remove the redundant duplicate RZ layer, replace the CZ ring entangler with a        │
│                          │ parametrized CRY ring (which does NOT commute with the surrounding RY/RZ layers, so it    │
│                          │ contributes genuine entangling expressivity), and close the ring (qubit 7 -> qubit 0) to  │
│                          │ give a fully connected entangling topology in one pass. This keeps the parameter count    │
│                          │ roughly the same (8 RY + 8 CRY + 8 RZ = 24 per block, versus 8+8+8=24 previously) while   │
│                          │ using every parameter meaningfully, which should improve expressivity per parameter,      │
│                          │ reduce the train/val gap, and potentially speed convergence.                              │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
