│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ double_block_diam_split_sharing                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The best-performing prior variant (score 0.89) used a *doubled* block (same shared        │
│                          │ parameter names repeated twice) to increase circuit depth without increasing parameter    │
│                          │ count, which visibly improved validation/test accuracy relative to the single-block       │
│                          │ variant (score 0.86, our current file). The current program regressed by dropping this    │
│                          │ depth-doubling structure.                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This edit reinstates the double-block depth strategy while keeping the useful additions   │
│                          │ from the current program (mirrored RX layer, mid-circuit mirrored RZ layer, skip-2        │
│                          │ entanglers, diameter entanglers). Additionally, per the recommendation, the antipodal     │
│                          │ ("diameter") CRZ layer is split into two hierarchically-shared parameters                 │
│                          │ (`crz_diam_even` for pairs [0,4] & [2,6], `crz_diam_odd` for pairs [1,5] & [3,7]) instead │
│                          │ of one single shared parameter, giving slightly richer long-range mixing at only +1       │
│                          │ parameter cost. The whole enriched block is then duplicated (reusing identical parameter  │
│                          │ names) to double depth exactly as in the strongest prior candidate, which should recover  │
│                          │ the depth-driven generalization benefit while retaining the extra mid-phase and diameter  │
│                          │ structure.                                                                                │
