                         Patch Metadata - Gen 52/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ This candidate crosses over the two strongest seeds (the "ring + single-shared-skip"      │
│                          │ variant that reached test-accuracy 0.91 with only 116 parameters, and the "ring + mixed   │
│                          │ skip/diamond" variant that reached 122 parameters but lower test accuracy) with the       │
│                          │ long-range "diamond" connectivity idea from the current program.                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Rationale:                                                                                │
│                          │ - The RY→ring-CRZ (alternating shared even/odd)→RX→skip-2-CRZ (single shared parameter)   │
│                          │ backbone from the best-performing seed is kept unchanged, since it already gives the best │
│                          │ test accuracy/generalization gap trade-off among the seeds.                               │
│                          │ - We add one extra, *maximally shared* long-range entangling layer (distance-4 "diamond"  │
│                          │ connections using CRX, a different rotation axis than the CRZ layers already present)     │
│                          │ with a *single* shared parameter, rather than the two separate parameters used in the     │
│                          │ lower-scoring seed. This keeps the parameter-efficiency advantage of strong sharing while │
│                          │ adding the extra long-range expressivity that the current (176-param, lower-score)        │
│                          │ program tried to exploit but diluted with too many independent RZ/diamond parameters.     │
│                          │ - The whole block is still duplicated (same parameter names) to double effective circuit  │
│                          │ depth without any extra parameter cost, exactly as in the two best-performing seeds.      │
│                          │ - Net effect: same backbone that reached 0.89 score / 0.91 test accuracy, plus one cheap  │
│                          │ (+1 parameter per repetition) long-range mixing layer that should help capture            │
│                          │ correlations beyond distance-2 without hurting the train/test gap, since it is a single,  │
│                          │ heavily shared parameter.                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This keeps the parameter count modest (20 unique params per outer repetition instead of   │
│                          │ 19 or 29), while combining the strongest structural elements of all provided programs.    │
